    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q13-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If two sides of a triangle have length 4, then what are the possible values for the length of the third side?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0<t<8$, where $t$ is the third side length.",
          "worked": "Let the third side have length $t$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}t&<4+4\\\\t&<8\\\\4+t&>4\\\\t&>0\\end{align*}$$\n\nTherefore, $0<t<8$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q13-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "How many different (non-congruent) triangles have integer side lengths and a perimeter of 10 units?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "2 triangles: side lengths $(2,4,4)$ and $(3,3,4)$ units.\n\nThe longest side must be less than half the perimeter.",
          "worked": "Write the integer side lengths as $a\\le b\\le c$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a+b+c&=10\\\\a+b&>c\\\\10-c&>c\\\\c&<5\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $c$ is the longest side, $3c\\ge10$. Hence $c=4$ and $a+b=6$. With $a\\le b\\le4$, the only pairs are $(a,b)=(2,4)$ and $(3,3)$. Both satisfy the triangle inequality.\n\nThere are 2 non-congruent triangles, with side lengths $(2,4,4)$ and $(3,3,4)$ units.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q13-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "The integer side lengths of a triangle are $x+2$, $2x+7$ and $4x+1$. Find the greatest possible value of $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=7$.\n\nApply all three triangle inequalities; the integer values satisfying them are $2\\le x\\le7$.",
          "worked": "Apply the triangle inequality to each side.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+2)+(2x+7)&>4x+1\\\\3x+9&>4x+1\\\\x&<8\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+2)+(4x+1)&>2x+7\\\\5x+3&>2x+7\\\\3x&>4\\\\x&>\\frac43\\end{align*}$$\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(2x+7)+(4x+1)&>x+2\\\\6x+8&>x+2\\\\5x&>-6\\\\x&>-\\frac65\\end{align*}$$\n\nTogether, $\\frac43<x<8$. Since $x+2$ is an integer, $x$ is an integer. The greatest possible value is $x=7$, giving positive side lengths $9$, $21$ and $29$, with $9+21>29$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 120
      }
    ]
  },
